1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,799
nvim in nvim: Neovim inside Neovim inside Neovim.

2
00:00:03,799 --> 00:00:10,113
A Vim like editor written in Lua, that runs on top of Neovim, and has its own configuration

3
00:00:10,113 --> 00:00:10,598
system.

4
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:17,116
Can Neovim's configuration system express a smaller Neovim, one that has a configuration system

5
00:00:17,116 --> 00:00:21,355
of its own, and can edit the configuration of another editor like itself?

6
00:00:21,355 --> 00:00:26,116
And can it do that using only the public A P I, no plugins and no extra processes?

7
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:30,750
Real Neovim loads one init dot lua, which boots editor A.

8
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:33,233
A spawns B, B spawns C, and C spawns D.

9
00:00:33,233 --> 00:00:35,144
They are not Neovim processes.

10
00:00:35,144 --> 00:00:41,066
Each is a logical editor implemented in Lua, with its own modes, registers, undo history, key

11
00:00:41,066 --> 00:00:45,843
mappings, and its own Lua environment where the word vim means this editor.

12
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:51,167
Neovim gives us three primitives: a key arriving, a buffer, and a float window.

13
00:00:51,167 --> 00:00:55,055
One on key hook swallows every key and routes it to the focused editor.

14
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:59,656
Each editor holds its own state, and one render module draws each editor as a float.

15
00:00:59,656 --> 00:01:04,585
Because a child editor needs the same three primitives, an editor can host another editor.

16
00:01:05,590 --> 00:01:12,028
The whole editor is 1,955 lines of Lua across 14 modules, with no third party plugins.

17
00:01:13,030 --> 00:01:18,494
Most of the logic sits in the editor core, the config loader, the mode state machine, motions

18
00:01:18,494 --> 00:01:19,316
and rendering.

19
00:01:19,316 --> 00:01:24,604
Operators and motions know nothing about each other, so adding one motion gives you delete

20
00:01:24,604 --> 00:01:26,895
change, yank and visual support for it.

21
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:29,752
The tests have four layers.

22
00:01:29,752 --> 00:01:32,221
67 Lua specs run in headless Neovim.

23
00:01:32,221 --> 00:01:38,393
66 of 66 end to end checks drive the real terminal U I in a pseudo terminal with raw bytes

24
00:01:38,393 --> 00:01:44,222
including the full story where editor B edits and saves C's config, and C reloads it.

25
00:01:44,222 --> 00:01:47,582
And 22 input research assertions back the design.

26
00:01:47,582 --> 00:01:48,474
All 155 pass.

27
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:50,135
The limits.

28
00:01:50,135 --> 00:01:55,881
Configs are trusted code: a private Lua environment isolates names and state, not capabilities.

29
00:01:55,881 --> 00:02:01,445
This is not full Vim: there is no regex search, folds, marks or macros, and undo is a linear

30
00:02:01,445 --> 00:02:01,989
snapshot.

31
00:02:01,989 --> 00:02:05,497
There is one physical terminal, so cursor shape is global.

32
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:11,632
Clone the repo, and run nvim dash u dot slash init dot lua to try it.

